  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... The mid and upper level low is centered near New \nYork City early this morning, with ample mid level moisture evident \non the latest water vapor loop. This feature will keep showers going \nearly this morning, with the activity tapering off and becoming more \nisolated in nature toward daybreak. Skies look to remain mostly \ncloudy through the morning hours. Instability increases this \nafternoon, and scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms are \nexpected to develop over the area. These showers and storm will move \nnort-northwest to southeast this afternoon; with the highest PoPs \nover the Catskills of east-central NY. At the same time, some drier \nmid level air will work its way into the region, allow for breaks of \nsun by mid to late afternoon (outside of the shower and t'storm \nactivity). This will allow temperatures to warm into the 70s \nareawide today, while it remain humid with dew points in the mid to \nupper 60s and pwats around 1.3 inches. MLCAPE values are expected to \nreach around 500 J/kg, but shear will be light, only around 20 kts. \nWe can't rule out some brief locally heavy rainfall with the storms \nthis afternoon, but overall severe weather is not expected today.\n\nThe rain showers end tonight and skies partially clear. Winds turn \nlight as high pressure gradually builds in from the west. This will \nbe a good setup to see valley fog development. Lows dip down into \nthe mid-50s and low 60s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nFriday is looking generally dry as mid and upper level ridging \nbuilds over the region. A 1015mb surface high will also be in place \nto keep skies mostly sunny. There is a slight chance for a late day \nshower or thunderstorm to pop up over the Catskills where the slow \ndeparting trough will still hold some influence. Temperatures warm \nto above average levels, into the upper 70s to mid-80s, with \ngenerally comfortable humidity levels.\n\nDry weather continues over the area Friday night and most of \nSaturday with the ridge drifting eastward. Can't rule out a late day \nshower or storm over the Central southern tier or Finger Lakes as \nthe next frontal system approaches from the west. Otherwise, skies \nare forecast to be partly sunny and temperatures a few degrees \nwarmer than Friday; well into the 80s areawide.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nBy Sunday, the next deep trough, front and low pressure center will \nbe moving through the area. Model consensus is good on the timing \nand position of these features. There will be ample moisture and \ninstability over the region as this system approaches; pwats rise \nbetween 1.8 to nearly 2.0 inches along with surface dew points \npotentially in the lower 70s at times. It is too early for specific \ndetails on exactly how much instability will be present, as that \nwill certainly depend on day time surface heating. There will be at \nleast some risk for excessive, heavy rainfall as this system \nmoves through. Current model guidance dries our area by Monday, \nwith drier air filtering in from north to south behind the \ndeparting front. Beyond this, expect a warming trend for the \nmiddle of next week with chances for afternoon and evening \nthunderstorms also in the forecast.",
